  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strengthening storm system organizes off the \nnorthern Mid-Atlantic coast Thursday into Friday.\n\nA vigorous upper-level trough continues to be forecast to \nevolve across the eastern U.S. later this week and into the \nweekend. This supports a surface low over the Mid-west that \nstrengthens as it tracks across portions of the Mid-Atlantic\ntonight. This low will will then transition its energy to a\ndeveloping area of low pressure offshore which will tend to \nconsolidate off the New Jersey coast on Thursday, and deepen as\nit tracks north-northeast toward the Canadian Maritimes on \nFriday into Saturday. While there are still some differences \nwith the handling of the embedded shortwaves, there is better \nmodel clustering with the surface low placement/track. Overall, \nit will be unsettled across our region as we go through Thursday\nand Friday.\n\nWhile showers are expected to develop over western portions of\nthe area late tonight and into early Thursday as the initial low\ntracks towards the area, the bulk of precipitation looks to \nhold off until the afternoon hours on Thursday. Any showers and\nrain may be moderate to occasionally heavy at times, where even\na few embedded thunderstorms cannot be ruled out. Upon latest \nguidance that arrived this afternoon, there has been an uptick \nin the instability department, albeit is very meager at best \naround 250-500 J/kg. Guidance has also hinted at a localized \nconvergence zone as the low transitions some energy offshore. \nWhile strongest flow aloft is displaced to our south and west, \nbulk shear values will be around 40 kt. As a result, SPC has \nintroduced a Marginal Risk for severe weather on Thursday \n(primarily in the early to mid-afternoon hours). Primary threat \nwould be localized damaging winds, but also cannot rule out a \nfew instances of hail with cold temperatures aloft. Overall, \nthis set-up is low CAPE, modest shear, so confidence in \noccurrence is less than usual.\n\nThe strength of the parent upper-level trough results in a \ncolder air mass moving in with time, however the initial air \nmass is not all that cold (the lack of a cold antecedent air \nmass). As the surface low consolidates off the northern Mid-\nAtlantic coast, the winds will turn more out of the northwest \nand this will increase the cold air advection. This will result \nin some colder air seeping southeastward later Thursday night \nbut especially Friday into Friday night. The air mass may become\nsufficiently cold enough mostly across our far northwestern \nzones for a rain/snow mix or even some snow across the higher \nelevations, confined particularly to the Pocono Plateau. Any\nsnowfall accumulation is expected to be less than an inch. Some\nelevated locations across the Lehigh Valley and Northwestern NJ\nmay see a few wet flakes mixing in, but no accumulations are\nexpected. Otherwise, a rain event is forecast. The amount of \nprecipitation (rain and/or snow) will depend on the ultimate \ntrack of the surface low and whether a deformation zones wraps \nback into our northern to central areas. At the moment, rainfall\namounts north of the PA Turnpike to I-195 corridor range around\nthe 0.75-1.25\" range while further south across Philly and \nsouth, we are expecting less than 0.75\" of rainfall, with less \nthan 0.25\" expected for the southern Delmarva.\n\nThe model consensus looks to be more of a northward positioning\nof the surface low, which if this ends up occurring would \nresult in less rainfall with a southward extent across our area.\nThe coastal impacts, including the potential for stronger \nonshore winds and also (minor) tidal flooding, are a little less\ncertain because the surface low track right over our area would\nlessen the residence time of the (stronger) onshore flow. Prior\nto the low fully consolidating offshore, some elevated \ninstability could get into especially portions of our coastal \nplain and therefore a rumble of thunder is possible later \nThursday night and Friday morning. The strongest winds with this\nlook to occur as the storm moves farther away and the cold air \nadvection then increases from the northwest. The main \nuncertainty is on how much precipitation occurs with this event \nregion-wide and the extent/amount of any higher elevation snow \n(mainly for the southern Poconos). \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minor tidal flooding expected with Thursday \nMorning's high tide.\n\nA brief period of onshore flow will result in some elevated\ntides. For the most part, tidal flooding will be relatively\nspotty for communities along the Atlantic Ocean and Delaware\nBay. More widespread tidal flooding though is currently forecast\nfor Barnegat Bay, which could result in some inundation of low\nlying areas and roadways Thursday Morning. As a result, a\nCoastal Flood Advisory was issued for Ocean County. \n\nNo other headlines are in place nor expected. No tidal flooding\nis expected along the tidal Delaware River and Chesapeake Bay. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A stretch of below normal temperatures begins \non",
